add(2016,1,4.5,'Geometry','Tangents|Similarity|Angle chasing',r'Circles $C_1,C_2$ touch externally at $S$, and the radius of $C_2$ is three times that of $C_1$. A common external tangent touches them at $P,Q$, respectively. Let $QT$ be a diameter of $C_2$, and let the internal bisector of $\angle SQT$ meet $ST$ at $R$. Prove that $QR=RT$.',None,'Equal tangents show that PSQ is right-angled and that P,S,T are collinear. The 1:3 radius ratio fixes the projection of S onto PQ, giving the 30–60–90 angles; the final angle-bisector step is short. The main choice is to introduce the common tangent at S.')
add(2016,2,6,'Number theory','Descent|Factor pairs|Reachability',r'Call positive integers $m,n$ linked if $m=ab$, $n=cd$ for positive integers satisfying $a+b=c+d$. Initially colour 3 and 5. Repeatedly, an uncoloured positive integer may be coloured whenever it is linked to a coloured integer. Which positive integers can eventually be coloured?',r'''Exactly the integers at least 3 can be coloured. The only factorisation of 1 has sum 2, and the only factorisation of 2 has sum 3; neither number is linked to a different number.

Write $x\sim y$ for linkage. We have $3\sim4\sim6$, since the relevant factor pairs are $(1,3),(2,2)$ and $(1,4),(2,3)$. Also $5$ is initially coloured.

For every $k\ge6$, there is a chain
$$k\sim6(k-5)\sim2(k-4)\sim k-3.$$
Indeed, the first step compares $(1,k)$ with $(6,k-5)$; the second compares $(3,2k-10)$ with $(1,2k-8)$; the third compares $(2,k-4)$ with $(1,k-3)$. In each case the factor sums agree and all factors are positive. Strong induction, using the coloured values 3,4,5, now colours every $k\ge6$ by reversing this chain.''','Direct factor-pair experiments do not readily expose all reachable numbers. The key is a three-link bridge reducing an arbitrary k to k−3; after it is found, induction and exclusion of 1 and 2 are straightforward.')
add(2016,5,7,'Combinatorics','Graph components|Invariants|Extremal constructions',r'An $n\times n$ grid contains $1,2,\ldots,n^2$ in row order. In one operation choose two cells sharing a side and add the same integer to both entries. Determine when all entries can be made zero, and in those cases find the minimum number of operations.',r'''It is possible exactly when $n$ is even; the minimum is $3n^2/4$.

For odd $n$, the initial sum $n^2(n^2+1)/2$ is odd, whereas every operation changes the sum by an even number, so zero is impossible.

Suppose $n$ is even. Draw an edge between cells on which an operation is performed. A connected component with $r$ cells needs at least $r-1$ distinct edges. Every component must have at least four cells: a singleton cannot change; a two-cell component preserves the difference of its unequal entries. A three-cell component is a path, whose alternating sum is invariant, so its middle initial entry $a$ would have to equal the sum of its neighbours. Both neighbours must therefore be smaller than $a$, hence must lie immediately above and left of it. Their values are $a-n,a-1$, so $a=(a-n)+(a-1)$ forces $a=n+1$, which has no left neighbour. This is impossible.

Thus there are at most $n^2/4$ components, and at least $n^2-n^2/4=3n^2/4$ distinct used edges, hence at least this many operations.

Partition the grid into $2\times2$ blocks. Each block has the form $\begin{smallmatrix}a&a+1\\b&b+1\end{smallmatrix}$. Subtract $a$ from its top pair, $b$ from its bottom pair, then 1 from its right pair. These three operations clear each block and attain the bound.''','The parity obstruction and block construction are accessible, but proving optimality requires turning operations into graph components. Eliminating every component of size at most three is a substantial structural insight that makes the sharp bound possible.')
add(2017,1,4.5,'Combinatorics','Parity|Chessboard|Constructive algorithms',r'A $2017\times2017$ chessboard has a knight in every cell of its first column. In a move, select two distinct knights and move both by legal knight moves. Determine all columns $k$ for which the knights can eventually occupy every cell of column $k$, one knight per cell.',r'''Exactly the odd columns are possible. Colour the board like a chessboard, with the upper left corner black. Every move changes the number of knights on black cells by $-2,0$, or 2. Initially this number is 1009, so it stays odd. An even column has only 1008 black cells and is impossible.

It remains to shift a whole column two places to the right. Use row and column coordinates. For the first three knights, initially at $(1,c),(2,c),(3,c)$, first move the first two to $(3,c+1),(3,c+2)$. Then move the first and third knights to $(1,c+2),(2,c+2)$. Every displacement is a knight move, and the three destination rows are occupied exactly once.

Pair the remaining rows consecutively. For each pair $(r,c),(r+1,c)$, move both knights to $(r+1,c+2),(r,c+2)$. Repeating this shift reaches every odd column.''','A chessboard parity invariant gives only necessity. The constructive half needs a special three-knight manoeuvre to handle the odd number of rows, followed by routine paired moves. Finding that small exceptional gadget raises it above an elementary invariant exercise.')
add(2017,3,6.5,'Geometry','Orthocentre|Cyclic quadrilaterals|Reflection|Auxiliary parallelogram',r'Let $H$ be the orthocentre of an acute triangle $ABC$. The circle through $B,H,C$ meets lines $AB,AC$ again at $D,E$, respectively. Put $P=HB\cap DE$ and $Q=HC\cap DE$. Line $AP$ meets the circle $AHB$ again at $X$, and line $AQ$ meets the circle $AHC$ again at $Y$. Prove that $XY\parallel BC$.',None,'Completing a parallelogram at A produces a point diametrically opposite H on the circle BHC and reveals HD=HE. Several cyclic angle relations then show that X and Y are reflections in AH. The auxiliary parallelogram and reflection viewpoint carry most of the difficulty.','The official text calls DE a segment, while its published solution diagram uses the full line; the intersection notation here uses the line, as needed in general configurations.')
add(2017,5,6.5,'Geometry','Arc midpoints|Incentre|Equal distances|Angle chasing',r'Points $A,B,N,C,D,M$ lie in that clockwise order on a circle, with $M,N$ the midpoints of the clockwise arcs $DA,BC$. Let $P=AC\cap BD$. Choose $Q$ on $MB$ so that $PQ\perp MN$, and choose $R$ on $MC$ so that $QB=RC$. Prove that $AC$ passes through the midpoint of $QR$.',None,'Intersecting MN with the two diagonals gives an isosceles triangle and identifies Q as the incentre of ABP. Equal right triangles then make Q and R equally distant from AC on opposite sides. Several arc and bisector relations must be coordinated before the final midpoint argument.')
add(2017,6,7,'Combinatorics','Games|Potential functions|Induction|Sharp bounds',r'For integers $n,m\ge2$, there are $m$ initially empty urns. On each round A adds one ball to each of two distinct urns, then B empties one urn. A wins if, after a turn of B, some urn contains $n$ balls. Find the smallest $m$ for which A can force a win.',r'''The answer is $m=2^{n-1}+1$.

For the lower bound, assign weight $0$ to an empty urn and $2^{k-1}$ to an urn with $k>0$ balls. B always empties an urn with the most balls. The total weight after B's turn never exceeds $m-1$: if A adds to two nonempty urns, the weight increase is at most twice the larger former weight, which B removes; if A uses one empty urn, the increase is at most $1+2^{k-1}\le2^k$, again removed by B. If A uses two empty urns, B removes at least 2 weight unless every urn has at most one ball, in which case the remaining weight is at most $m-1$. Thus $m\le2^{n-1}$ prevents any surviving urn from reaching weight $2^{n-1}$.

Now let $m=2^{n-1}+1$. By repeatedly filling two empty urns, A can obtain at least $2^{n-1}$ nonempty urns. More generally, suppose $2r$ urns have at least $k$ balls. In each of the next $r$ rounds A chooses two surviving urns from this group, prioritising those still having exactly $k$ balls. At least $r+1$ urns survive before the last of these rounds, so two are always available. Whenever at least two still have exactly $k$ balls, both are raised; if only one does, it is raised immediately. Hence after $r$ rounds none of the surviving group remains at level $k$, while at least $r$ urns survive. A has therefore produced at least $r$ urns with $k+1$ balls.

Starting with $2^{n-1}$ nonempty urns and repeatedly applying this step gives one urn with at least $n$ balls. Taking the first round on which any surviving urn reaches that level gives exactly $n$ balls, as required.''','A sharp answer needs both an exponential-weight defensive invariant and a matching strategy that halves the number of maintained urns while raising their level. Choosing and checking this potential, then making the adversarial induction precise, places it at the upper edge of the range.','Original exposition makes explicit how A selects urns in the constructive induction, a detail compressed in the organizer solution.')
